\details{
Random-effect terms use lme4 syntax and smooths use mgcv syntax, so a model
can mix them freely:

\preformatted{
  y ~ 1                                      intercept only
  y ~ x1 + grp                               ordinary regression
  y ~ x1 + grp + (1 | subj)                  random intercept
  y ~ x1 + time + (1 + time | subj)          random intercept and slope
  y ~ x1 + s(xs, k = 10) + (1 | subj)        penalised smooth of xs
  y ~ x1 + t2(lon, lat) + (1 | site)         2-D surface (spatial)
}

Two rules for smooth terms. Use \code{t2()} rather than \code{te()} for tensor
products, because \code{te()} cannot be converted to the mixed-model form this
package relies on. And write smooths \strong{unqualified} -- \code{s(x)}, not
\code{mgcv::s(x)} -- because mgcv identifies them by name, so a namespaced call
would be mistaken for an ordinary predictor. This matches \code{mgcv::gam()}.

The outcome may be a factor or a character vector. Its levels set the category
labels used throughout the output.

Random-effect bars are extracted with \code{lme4::findbars()} and smooths with
\code{mgcv::interpret.gam()}, then reparameterised by \code{ilm_smooth()} so that the
unpenalised part of each smooth joins the fixed effects and the penalised part
becomes a random term.
}
\section{Simple models get exact inference}{

A gaussian model with no random or smooth terms is an ordinary linear model.
In that case there is nothing to integrate out, so illume reports \strong{exact}
t tests on \code{n - p} degrees of freedom and F tests in \code{\link[=ilm_anova]{ilm_anova()}}, rather
than the large-sample normal and chi-square approximations it must use when
random effects are present. Coefficients, standard errors, the residual
standard deviation and the F tests then agree with \code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}} and
\code{car::Anova()} to numerical precision.

This applies only where an exact reference genuinely exists. A Poisson or
binomial model without random effects is still a GLM, with no exact
small-sample analogue, so it keeps z and chi-square.
}

\section{Reading the coefficients}{

Categories are coded \strong{sum-to-zero}, so a coefficient is that category's
deviation from the average across categories, \emph{not} a contrast against a
baseline. \code{summary()} prints a reminder, because this is easy to misread if
you are used to \code{\link[nnet:multinom]{nnet::multinom()}}.
}

\section{Always read the checks}{

This model class fails quietly: a fit can return sensible-looking
coefficients while its covariance matrix is unusable, making the standard
errors meaningless. \code{summary()} prints the check verdicts for that reason,
and \code{fit$checks} holds the full table with a reason and a suggested remedy
for anything that is not \code{"OK"}.
}

